## Review process

Modeled on how the Claude Code team does code review: most changes merge with at most one human in the loop, and that must not make reverts or incidents more frequent. Tiers, people and command names live in `{{ configPath }}`; one-time setup and the FAQ are in `docs/dev-process/review-setup.md`.

1. **Check your own work before opening a {{ pr }}**: run the project's checks and the tests related to your change locally, and paste the commands and their output into the {{ pr }} (no output, no proof). Then run `/code-review high`{{#if policy.securityPaths}}; if you touch a security review path ({{#each policy.securityPaths}}`{{ this }}`{{#unless @last}}, {{/unless}}{{/each}}), run `/security-review` as well{{/if}}. Write what these found and what you fixed into the {{ pr }} description, not just the chat.
2. **Keep {{ pr }}s small**: split non-doc changes over about {{ policy.sizeThresholdLines }} lines into a stack of {{ pr }}s. Raise questions of direction in an issue first instead of finding out when the {{ pr }} is done.
3. **Fill in the whole {{ pr }} template**: what changed, verification (commands and results), risk tier, what happens if it breaks.
4. **Answer every {{ ai }} comment**: fix it, or reply starting with {{#each policy.threadReplyTags}}`{{ this }}`{{#unless @last}} / {{/unless}}{{/each}} and a reason, then resolve the thread. Unresolved threads block the merge, and the tags are how {{ ai }}'s false-positive rate is measured.
5. **`{{ policy.names.gateContext }}` sets the risk tier from the change, not the author**:
   - Touching a path that always needs a human (`highPaths` in `{{ configPath }}`) is high; touching only paths like docs (`lowOnlyPaths`) is low; everything else is medium. Non-doc changes over {{ policy.sizeThresholdLines }} lines are at least medium.{{#if classifier}} An AI classifier gives a second opinion, but it can only raise the tier, never lower it.{{/if}}
   - People needed: {{#if policy.requiredHumans.low}}{{ policy.requiredHumans.low }}{{else}}none{{/if}} for low, {{ policy.requiredHumans.medium }} for medium, {{ policy.requiredHumans.high }} for high. The count includes the author's own sign-off; the rest are approvals from others on the head commit. An author who is not on the trusted list needs {{ policy.untrustedExtraApprovals }} more.
   - Every tier needs a real {{ ai }} review of the head commit (notices about quota or errors don't count). When {{ ai }} is down or out of quota, someone with write access comments `{{ policy.commands.waiver }} <head sha>` on the {{ pr }}: the gate stops waiting for {{ ai }} and instead raises the number of approvals needed from others by {{ policy.aiReviewWaiverExtraApprovals }}. Like a sign-off, a waiver covers only that commit; new commits need a new one.
6. **Only the human author signs off**: comment `{{ policy.commands.signoff }} <head sha>` on the {{ pr }} to say you have read the evidence and own this change. New commits need a new sign-off. **Claude must never post `{{ policy.commands.signoff }}` or `{{ policy.commands.waiver }}`, and must never approve a {{ pr }} on anyone's behalf.**
{{#if queue.self}}
7. **Merge only through the queue**: add the `{{ policy.names.queueLabel }}` label whenever you like. The queue waits until `{{ policy.names.gateContext }}` is satisfied (including the {{ ai }} review), re-runs CI on "latest `{{ defaultBranch }}` + this {{ pr }}", and merges only when that is green. Never merge by hand: only the queue can set the required `{{ policy.names.queueContext }}` check to success, and every required check is bound to GitHub Actions, so a status posted by hand does not count.
{{else}}
7. **Merge only through the queue**: once the gate is satisfied, add the {{ pr }} to the merge queue (**Merge when ready** on the {{ pr }} page). The queue re-runs the required checks on "latest `{{ defaultBranch }}` + this {{ pr }}" and merges only when they are green. Never merge by hand: every required check is bound to GitHub Actions, so a status posted by hand does not count.
{{/if}}
8. **At most {{ policy.reviewRoundCap }} review rounds**: if {{ ai }} is still raising new problems in round {{ policy.reviewRoundCap }}, stop and rethink the approach (is the direction right, should it be split?) instead of fixing one round after another.
9. **You land it, you own it**: when a change causes a problem, its author follows up; after a release, the on-call person of the week watches for feedback, see `docs/dev-process/on-call.md`.
10. **Emergency bypass**: only repository admins can bypass the rules to merge a {{ pr }}. When they do, they write the reason in the {{ pr }} and complete the review afterwards.
11. **Never route around the gate**: Claude must not post commit statuses by hand to turn a check green, nor edit the CI workflows, `{{ configPath }}` or `{{ engine }}/` to do so. When the gate is red, work through the "Still missing" list in its pinned comment; if you think the gate is wrong, say so in the {{ pr }} and ask a human to change the rules.

## Changing the review rules themselves

- Process files (the CI workflows, `CLAUDE.md` at any depth, `.claude/`, `.mcp.json`, `{{ configPath }}`, `{{ engine }}/`, `{{ instructionsPath }}`, `{{ securityPath }}`) are always high and covered by CODEOWNERS: {{ ai }} reads the review instructions from the {{ pr }} branch, and the Claude Code in the review job also reads `CLAUDE.md` and `.claude/` (whose hooks can run commands), so a {{ pr }} that changes them must be reviewed by another person.
- The gate and the queue read `{{ configPath }}` from the target branch (usually `{{ defaultBranch }}`), so changes to tiers, people or commands take effect only after they merge; a {{ pr }} cannot change its own gate.
- `{{ configPath }}`, CODEOWNERS, the review instructions and the workflows are rendered by the dev-process plugin from `.dev-process/answers.json`: to change the rules, edit the answers and re-render rather than editing generated files. CODEOWNERS mirrors `highPaths`, and the consistency check (`node {{ engine }}/cli/doctor.mjs`) compares the two.
- When {{ ai }} keeps missing something or keeps flagging non-problems, change `{{ instructionsPath }}` or `{{ securityPath }}` and add a case to `{{ policy.paths.evalCases }}`.
